      "message": {
        "content": "{\n  \"shared_scope\": {\n    \"review_title\": \"Gut Microbiome Modulation of Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Efficacy in Solid Tumours: Mechanisms and Clinical Evidence\",\n    \"central_question\": \"How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect?\",\n    \"provisional_scope\": \"This review synthesizes evidence on the role of the gut microbiome in modulating immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) efficacy across solid tumours. It covers clinical associations (antibiotic exposure timing, FMT outcomes), microbial signatures (taxa, metabolites), mechanistic pathways (immune modulation, metabolic signaling, barrier integrity), and therapeutic interventions. The scope excludes hematological malignancies unless mechanistic insights are directly transferable to solid tumours. Emphasis is placed on human clinical data over preclinical models, with explicit notation of evidence grades (observational vs. interventional).\",\n    \"scope_adjustments_from_supplements\": [\n      \"Explicitly state that no completed Phase III randomized controlled trials of microbiome interventions in ICI-treated solid tumour patients exist as of late 2025; current evidence relies on Phase I/II trials and observational data.\",\n      \"Acknowledge that human tumour microenvironment metabolite concentrations (inosine, SCFAs) are not directly quantified in available studies; plasma stratification data and proxy biomarkers (UBA6 expression) are used instead.\",\n      \"Distinguish tumor-type specificity in antibiotic sensitivity (head/neck, RCC, breast show stronger post-ICI antibiotic effects; lung, melanoma show weaker associations in large cohort data).\",\n      \"Note that FMT Phase II data shows promising ORR (70-80% in NSCLC/melanoma) but elevated irAE rates in dual-ICI contexts (65% grade ≥3 AEs in melanoma cohort).\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"shared_outline\": {\n    \"chapters\": [\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH01\",\n        \"title\": \"Clinical Landscape: Antibiotics, FMT, and Observational Data\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Evaluate the strength of clinical evidence linking microbiome perturbations to ICI outcomes, distinguishing observational associations from interventional data.\",\n        \"question\": \"What is the current state of clinical evidence regarding microbiome modulation of ICI efficacy, and what are the evidence grade limitations?\",\n        \"evidence_basis\": [\n          \"Large-scale observational data on antibiotic timing (21,108 patients; post-ICI exposure HR 1.27 for mortality)\",\n          \"Phase II FMT-LUMINate trial outcomes (ORR 80% NSCLC, 75% melanoma; 65% grade ≥3 AEs in dual-ICI arm)\",\n          \"Explicit absence of Phase III RCTs confirmed through multiple supplement searches\"\n        ],\n        \"scope_change_from_supplement\": \"Added explicit statement on Phase III RCT absence; refined antibiotic timing claims to distinguish pre- vs. post-ICI windows with tumor-type stratification.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH02\",\n        \"title\": \"Microbial Signatures: Taxa and Metabolites\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Identify specific microbial features associated with response or resistance, noting quantification limitations in human samples.\",\n        \"question\": \"Which specific taxa and metabolites predict ICI response in solid tumours, and what are the measurement constraints?\",\n        \"evidence_basis\": [\n          \"Plasma inosine stratification correlating with OS in RCC (median OS 33 vs. 22 months high vs. low)\",\n          \"SCFA fecal levels associated with PFS in prospective cohorts (52 patients)\",\n          \"No absolute human TME concentration data available; reliance on stratification and proxy biomarkers\"\n        ],\n        \"scope_change_from_supplement\": \"Added explicit limitation on metabolite quantification; cannot report μM/ng/mL values for human TME inosine or SCFAs.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH03\",\n        \"title\": \"Mechanistic Pathways: Immune, Metabolic, and Barrier Functions\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Elucidate the biological mechanisms mediating microbiome-ICI interactions, distinguishing preclinical mechanisms from human validation.\",\n        \"question\": \"What molecular and cellular mechanisms mediate the effect of the microbiome on ICI efficacy, and which have human validation?\",\n        \"evidence_basis\": [\n          \"Inosine-UBA6 inhibition pathway enhancing MHC-I expression (mouse models; human UBA6 protein expression correlation)\",\n          \"SCFA-mediated HDAC inhibition and GPR signaling (preclinical; limited human mechanistic data)\",\n          \"Gut barrier integrity markers (sMAdCAM-1) correlating with PFS in Phase III JAVELIN Renal 101 biomarker analysis\"\n        ],\n        \"scope_change_from_supplement\": \"Clarified that UBA6 mechanism has human protein expression support but no direct human metabolite concentration validation.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH04\",\n        \"title\": \"Therapeutic Interventions: FMT, Probiotics, and Diet\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Assess the feasibility and efficacy of microbiome-targeted interventions, noting trial phase limitations.\",\n        \"question\": \"Can microbiome interventions reliably enhance ICI efficacy in clinical practice, and what is the current trial phase status?\",\n        \"evidence_basis\": [\n          \"FMT Phase II trials (FMT-LUMINate: ORR 70-80%; safety signals vary by ICI regimen)\",\n          \"Engineered bacteria Phase I data (SYNB1891, MRx0518 safety signals)\",\n          \"No Phase III efficacy data; regulatory challenges for Live Biotherapeutic Products\"\n        ],\n        \"scope_change_from_supplement\": \"Explicitly framed as Phase I/II evidence only; cannot claim Phase III validation exists.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH05\",\n        \"title\": \"Limitations, Heterogeneity, and Future Directions\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Address evidence gaps, methodological challenges, and research priorities based on confirmed absence of Phase III data and metabolite quantification.\",\n        \"question\": \"What are the major limitations in current research and what is needed for clinical translation?\",\n        \"evidence_basis\": [\n          \"Confirmed absence of Phase III RCTs for microbiome interventions in ICI-treated solid tumours\",\n          \"Lack of standardized metabolite quantification in human TME\",\n          \"Tumor-type and ICI-regimen specificity in intervention outcomes\",\n          \"Regulatory and standardization barriers for microbiome therapeutics\"\n        ],\n        \"scope_change_from_supplement\": \"Elevated Phase III absence from 'search gap' to 'confirmed field limitation'; added metabolite quantification as priority research need.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"review_argument\": {\n    \"thesis\": \"The gut microbiome modulates ICI efficacy in solid tumours through immune, metabolic, and barrier-mediated mechanisms, but clinical translation remains constrained by the absence of Phase III randomized trial evidence and limited human metabolite quantification data.\",\n    \"supporting_lines\": [\n      \"Observational data consistently links post-ICI antibiotic exposure to reduced survival (HR 1.27 in 21,108-patient cohort), with tumor-type specificity suggesting differential microbiome dependency.\",\n      \"Phase II FMT trials demonstrate promising efficacy signals (ORR 70-80%) but reveal regimen-dependent safety risks (65% grade ≥3 AEs in dual-ICI melanoma), indicating intervention complexity.\",\n      \"Metabolite mechanisms (inosine-UBA6, SCFA-HDAC) have preclinical validation and human biomarker correlations, but lack direct human TME concentration measurements needed for therapeutic targeting.\",\n      \"The field currently lacks Phase III RCT confirmation of reproducible survival benefit from microbiome interventions, limiting clinical readiness despite mechanistic plausibility.\"\n    ],\n    \"evidence_grade_statement\": \"Clinical associations (antibiotics, metabolite correlations) are observational (Grade C); FMT and engineered bacteria interventions are Phase I/II only (Grade B); no Phase III RCT evidence exists (Grade insufficient for clinical recommendation).\"\n  },\n  \"source_selection_principles\": [\n    \"Prioritize human clinical data for efficacy claims; use preclinical data primarily for mechanistic explanation with explicit species notation.\",\n    \"Distinguish observational associations (antibiotics, metabolite stratification) from interventional data (FMT trials) when discussing efficacy.\",\n    \"Exclude studies focused solely on hematological malignancies unless mechanistic insights are explicitly linked to solid tumour pathways.\",\n    \"Flag studies lacking adjustment for clinical confounders (infection severity, performance status) when citing antibiotic effects.\",\n    \"Do not generalize metabolite effects without specifying context (ICI type, concentration proxy, tumor type).\",\n    \"When Phase III RCT data is absent, cite review sources confirming the gap rather than implying search failure.\",\n    \"Retain original study limitations (sample size, stratification vs. absolute concentrations) in all mechanistic claims.\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_limits\": [\n    {\n      \"limit_id\": \"phase3_rct_absence\",\n      \"description\": \"No completed Phase III randomized controlled trials of microbiome interventions in ICI-treated solid tumour patients with published primary efficacy outcomes (OS/PFS/ORR) exist as of late 2025.\",\n      \"impact_on_conclusions\": \"Cannot recommend microbiome interventions as standard care; must frame FMT and engineered bacteria as investigational.\",\n      \"source_confirmation\": \"Multiple supplement searches (CorpusId:285298099 review) explicitly confirm Phase III absence; not a search failure but a field limitation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"limit_id\": \"metabolite_quantification_gap\",\n      \"description\": \"Specific quantitative concentration values (μM, ng/mL) of inosine and SCFAs in human tumour microenvironment or serum correlating with ICI outcomes are not available in reviewed materials.\",\n      \"impact_on_conclusions\": \"Mechanistic claims about metabolite effects must note reliance on stratification data and proxy biomarkers (UBA6 expression) rather than absolute concentrations.\",\n      \"source_confirmation\": \"CorpusId:252309032 and related searches confirm plasma stratification only; human TME tissue concentrations not measured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"limit_id\": \"tumor_type_heterogeneity\",\n      \"description\": \"Antibiotic sensitivity and FMT outcomes vary by tumor type (head/neck, RCC, breast show stronger antibiotic effects; lung, melanoma show variable FMT toxicity).\",\n      \"impact_on_conclusions\": \"Cannot make universal claims about microbiome-ICI interactions; must specify tumor-type context in all efficacy statements.\",\n      \"source_confirmation\": \"82ebda249f3476e1948f523a9219bd678f5165c3 cohort analysis shows tumor-type stratified HRs; 071823eff68d7dbea395490dd4643170131b5f6a shows regimen-dependent FMT toxicity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"limit_id\": \"confounder_adjustment\",\n      \"description\": \"Observational antibiotic studies lack granular ECOG status and PD-L1 expression data in some cohorts, potentially affecting confounder control.\",\n      \"impact_on_conclusions\": \"Antibiotic-ICI associations should be framed as correlations with noted residual confounding risk.\",\n      \"source_confirmation\": \"82ebda249f3476e1948f523a9219bd678f5165c3 notes missing ECOG/PD-L1 in EHR data.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manuscript_parts_plan\": {\n    \"context\": \"Oncologists, immunologists, and translational researchers seeking to understand the current evidence base for microbiome modulation of ICI efficacy in solid tumours, including clinical implications and research gaps. Readers require clear distinction between observational associations and interventional evidence, explicit notation of Phase III trial absence, and mechanistic claims tied to human validation status.\",\n    \"abstract\": {\n      \"purpose\": \"Provide a concise summary of the review's scope, key findings on modulation and mechanisms, and the current evidence grade including Phase III absence.\",\n      \"focus\": [\n        \"Summary of clinical associations (antibiotic timing post-ICI HR 1.27, FMT Phase II ORR 70-80%)\",\n        \"Key microbial taxa and metabolites identified (inosine, SCFAs) with quantification limitations noted\",\n        \"Major mechanistic pathways (UBA6 inhibition, immune priming) with human validation status\",\n        \"Explicit statement on lack of Phase III RCTs and investigational status of interventions\"\n      ],\n      \"boundary\": [\n        \"Exclude detailed methodological descriptions of included studies\",\n        \"Do not present observational associations as causal facts\",\n        \"Do not claim Phase III evidence exists\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": {\n        \"mode\": \"standalone\",\n        \"anchor\": \"beginning\"\n      },\n      \"finalize_from\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH02\",\n        \"CH03\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"introduction\": {\n      \"purpose\": \"Define the clinical problem of ICI resistance, introduce the gut microbiome as a modifiable factor, and outline the review's objectives and structure with evidence grade expectations.\",\n      \"focus\": [\n        \"ICI efficacy limitations in solid tumours (primary/acquired resistance rates)\",\n        \"Rationale for microbiome research (observational signals from antibiotic and FMT studies)\",\n        \"Review scope (solid tumours, ICIs, gut microbiome; exclude hematological malignancies)\",\n        \"Outline of chapters with explicit note on Phase III evidence absence\"\n      ],\n      \"boundary\": [\n        \"Do not review basic ICI mechanisms in depth\",\n        \"Exclude hematological malignancies from scope definition\",\n        \"Do not overstate microbiome contribution relative to other resistance mechanisms\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": {\n        \"mode\": \"standalone\",\n        \"anchor\": \"beginning\"\n      },\n      \"finalize_from\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"purpose\": \"Synthesize the strength of evidence, highlight clinical implications (antibiotic stewardship, FMT potential), and emphasize critical research gaps (Phase III trials, metabolite quantification) without overstating translational readiness.\",\n      \"focus\": [\n        \"Summary of evidence quality (observational Grade C, interventional Phase I/II Grade B, no Phase III)\",\n        \"Clinical recommendations (cautious antibiotic use post-ICI, FMT as investigational only)\",\n        \"Research priorities (Phase III RCTs, standardized metabolite quantification, tumor-type stratification)\",\n        \"Final statement on translational readiness (mechanistic plausibility confirmed, clinical validation pending)\"\n      ],\n      \"boundary\": [\n        \"Do not propose unvalidated interventions as standard care\",\n        \"Avoid overstating mechanistic certainty from preclinical data\",\n        \"Do not imply Phase III trials are imminent without confirmed protocol data\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": {\n        \"mode\": \"standalone\",\n        \"anchor\": \"end\"\n      },\n      \"finalize_from\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH04\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to finalize the first shared outline for a review on \"Gut Microbiome Modulation of Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Efficacy in Solid Tumours: Mechanisms and Clinical Evidence\".\n\nI need to:\n1. Create a `shared_scope` that reflects the actual level-1 supplement and directed reading results\n2. Create a `shared_outline` with ordered chapters based on the evidence available\n3. Write a `review_argument` that synthesizes the main thesis\n4. Define `source_selection_principles` for how sources will be used\n5. Identify `unresolved_limits` based on what the supplement searches found (or didn't find)\n6. Include a complete `manuscript_parts_plan` with context, abstract, introduction, and conclusion\n\nKey findings from the tool results:\n1. **Phase III RCT Gap (case_E_phase3_rct)**: Multiple searches confirmed NO completed Phase III RCTs exist for microbiome interventions in ICI-treated solid tumors. The evidence is limited to Phase I/II trials (FMT, engineered bacteria) and observational data. This is a critical gap that must be explicitly stated, not treated as a search failure.\n\n2. **Inosine/TME Concentration Gap (case_C2_inosine_tme)**: Partial fulfillment. Human plasma inosine levels correlate with ICI outcomes (CheckMate 025 re-analysis), but specific quantitative concentrations (μM, ng/mL) are not reported in the available texts—only stratification by median. No direct human tumor tissue inosine measurements exist; UBA6 protein expression is used as a proxy. SCFA quantitative data in human TME is also absent.\n\n3. **Directed Reading Results**:\n   - **Antibiotic Timing (82ebda249f3476e1948f523a9219bd678f5165c3)**: Post-ICI antibiotic exposure (within 60 days) significantly increases mortality (HR 1.27), while pre-ICI exposure shows weaker association. Tumor-type specificity exists (head/neck, RCC, breast more sensitive; lung, melanoma less sensitive in this cohort).\n   - **Inosine Mechanism (CorpusId:252309032)**: Inosine inhibits UBA6 in tumor cells, enhancing MHC-I and antigen presentation. Human plasma inosine correlates with OS in RCC (33 vs 22 months median OS for high vs low), but absolute concentrations not reported.\n   - **FMT Trial (071823eff68d7dbea395490dd4643170131b5f6a)**: Phase II FMT-LUMINate trial shows ORR 80% in NSCLC (FMT + pembrolizumab) and 75% in melanoma (FMT + nivolumab + ipilimumab). Higher irAE rates in melanoma dual-ICI arm (65% grade ≥3 AEs).\n\n4. **Scope Adjustments Needed**:\n   - Cannot claim Phase III evidence exists; must explicitly state the absence.\n   - Cannot provide specific metabolite concentrations for human TME; must note this as a methodological limitation.\n   - Must distinguish observational (antibiotics, metabolite correlations) from interventional (FMT Phase II) evidence.\n   - Must note tumor-type specificity in antibiotic sensitivity and FMT outcomes.\n\n5. **Chapter Organization**:\n   - CH01: Clinical Evidence (antibiotics, FMT trials) - now includes explicit statement on Phase III absence\n   - CH02: Microbial Signatures (taxa, metabolites) - must note concentration data limitations\n   - CH03: Mechanisms (immune, metabolic, barrier) - UBA6 pathway, T cell priming\n   - CH04: Interventions (FMT, probiotics, diet) - Phase II data only, no Phase III\n   - CH05: Limitations & Future Directions - Phase III gap, standardization needs, metabolite quantification needs\n\n6. **Manuscript Parts Plan**:\n   - Context: Oncologists, immunologists, translational researchers\n   - Abstract: Summary of evidence grade, key findings, Phase III absence\n   - Introduction: Clinical problem, microbiome rationale, scope\n   - Conclusion: Evidence synthesis, clinical implications, research priorities\n\nLet me construct the JSON response with all required fields.",
